<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siemens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Siemens &amp; Halske</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siemens is a German engineering company founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1847 by Werner von Siemens and Johann Georg Halske as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> manufacturer. It built telegraph lines across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and expanded into electric power, lighting and railways, becoming one of the largest electrical firms in the world. It remains one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s leading industrial companies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siemens.jpg" alt="Siemens" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Falbisoner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Wings,_Siemens_HQ_Munich,_April_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Siemens &amp; Halske was founded by Werner von Siemens and Johann Georg Halske on 1 October 1847. Its first product was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> that used a needle to point to the letters of a message instead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morse-code">Morse code</a>, and its first workshop opened on 12 October. In 1848 the company built the first long-distance telegraph line in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, 500 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main. Werner's brother Carl Wilhelm Siemens began to represent it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1850. In 1867 it completed the Indo-European telegraph line of over 11,000 km from London to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The firm was incorporated in 1897 and in 1903 merged part of its activities with Schuckert &amp; Co. of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> to form Siemens-Schuckert. In 1919 it helped found the Osram lightbulb company, and in the 1920s and 1930s it began to make radios, television sets and electron <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">microscopes</a>. It built the Ardnacrusha hydroelectric station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-shannon">River Shannon</a> in the Irish Free State in the 1920s. In 1932 a third parent company, Siemens-Reiniger-Werke, was formed.</p><p>From 1933 to 1945 Siemens-Schuckert exploited the forced labour of deported people. It owned a plant at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>, and a Siemens factory stood in front of the Ravensbrück concentration camp, where women were forced to work. By the end of 1944 the company ran almost 400 relocated plants because Allied air raids had destroyed factories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and other cities.</p><p>In 1962 Siemens &amp; Halske agreed to make up to 7 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutsche-mark">Deutsche Marks</a> available to Jewish former forced labourers. From new bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> the firm made computers, semiconductors and pacemakers in the 1950s, and in 1966 the three parent companies merged to form Siemens AG. Beginning in 2005 a multinational bribery scandal led to a settlement in December 2008, in which the company paid about $1.6 billion to the US and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Siemens is incorporated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with its headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. Its principal divisions are Digital Industries, Smart Infrastructure and Mobility, with a financial services arm, and it has repeatedly sold off parts of its business, among them Infineon Technologies in 1999, Osram in 2013, Siemens Healthineers in 2017 and Siemens Energy in 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siemens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The chemical industry, led by BASF, Bayer, and Hoechst, supplied roughly eighty to ninety percent of the world's synthetic dyes by 1913, and German firms such as <b>Siemens</b> and AEG accounted for nearly half of world exports of electrical goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
